hey guys Greg knuckles here with string  theory calm and today we have the first  installment of a new series I'm doing  for this channel theoretically I'm going  to be back on YouTube more I know that's  something I've said in the past hasn't  really stuck don't really like talking  to a camera but I'll be a big boy and  suck it up  working on finishing up the second  editions of my books the art and science  of lifting right now and that's what's  eating up most of my time so I don't  have all that much time to put out you  know kind of link the articles that are  typical for string theory just they take  a long time to write it's a lot quicker  and easier to put out videos so that's  that's what I'm going to be doing for a  while just to you know try to keep  putting out content not just drop off  the face of the earth entirely so how  this is going to work is theoretically  every day that's what I'm aiming for I'm  going to put out a video just answering  the best question that I've been asked  in the past 24 hours these ice or this  one i solicited from facebook but in the  future just comment on this video with a  question you would like answered and it  may be the one that i address in the  next video um but yeah so and for the  most part i'm going to be looking for  you know more practical questions you  know there will be some theoretical  stuff mechanistic stuff but for the most  part these are going to be videos  addressing kind of more specific  questions about how you can improve your  training so starting off with this one  the question was basically what's my  take on the overhead press military  press shoulder press whatever you call  it for bench press is does it have a  good transfer is that a good accessory  lift for the bench press  um and so  Oh in general I do think that the  overhead press is a good movement just  in its own right in general but I think  for most people it's not it's not that  good of an accessory lift for the bench  press but for some people it can be a  really good lift so kind of to address  those people first for most people the  sticking point of the bench press is in  the mid range that's where most people  tend to miss a bench press not that many  people just miss right coming off their  chest not a ton of people miss it  lockout most people miss through the mid  range however for the people who do miss  just when the bars breaking their chest  so maybe it doesn't move at all maybe it  just moves an inch or two but doesn't  you know really get to that mid-range  position that is typically going to be  limited by shoulder strength especially  when you're benching with more of a  powerlifting style bench press touching  fairly low on your chest the reason is  this essentially the distance between  the bar and your shoulder is what's  going to dictate how challenging the  lift is for your anterior deltoids so  you know essentially a bench bar path  you're going to touch here and the bar  is going to come back as you press it  out so when the bars here there's a  longer distance between where the bar is  and where your shoulder is then it  really any other point in the movement  so right there the very bottom of the  lift is going to be the most challenging  position for your shoulders and like I  said most people can get the bar moving  and stall somewhere in the mid range so  this would be for those people who you  know like everyone's going to miss right  off their chest if they take way too big  of a jump but you know let's say you got  315 completed the press put 320 325 on  barges and again moving at all  so that that would be the people that  this applies to for for those people the  shoulder press overhead press can be a  really really good accessory movement  because it is just a really good  movement for strengthening your  shoulders  if you do have that characteristic  sticking point in the bench if you do  miss at the very bottom just  strengthening your shoulders is probably  going to make a pretty big difference  for your bench so if that is where you  miss it I would recommend the overhead  press the shoulder press as an accessory  movement for your bench however most  people that's probably not where they're  going to miss you know and just simply  the harder you're training your shoulder  press that that's going to detract from  how hard you can train your bench so you  know just in terms of specificity if  you're you know if you're substituting  out maybe five sets of bench will week  for five sets of overhead press a week  you'd probably be better off doing five  more sets of bench instead now it is  certainly true that if you get a really  really really good overhead press just  simply because you'll be so strong at  pressing in general that you'll probably  also have a pretty respectable bench  from that you see that with a fair  amount of weight lifters and some strong  men most strongmen still train the bench  press but some of them don't really they  do most of their work overhead and they  can still put up respectable bench  numbers if they want to all I'm saying  here is that for most people that's  probably not the most efficient way to  improve your bench press so kind of my  own personal anecdote on this I  traditionally haven't put all that much  work into my overhead press and at one  point I was benching 435 I believe and  my max strict press was 185 which was  terrible so you know I just wanted to  get my strict press up because I was so  bad at it  so I trained it pretty hard for a while  and I didn't slack off on my bench  training it wasn't like I was doing this  at solely at the expense of benching I  was still training my bench and I put 90  pounds on my strict press I got it  up to 275 which relative to my bench  still wasn't a great overhead press  number but it was at least not like you  know he wasn't it wasn't as horrible as  it was before  and despite putting you know 90 pounds  on my overhead press when I started  focusing much harder on the bench again  when I came back to it  my bench was still 435 and it wasn't it  wasn't like a delayed transfer either I  put a really you know pretty pretty hard  dedicated training cycle into my bench  to see if the improved shoulder strength  would kind of pay off down down the line  once some of that fatigue was  ameliorated and now like six weeks later  I put I think maybe five pounds on my  bench so you know if anyone were a  candidate for improving their shoulder  press to pay big dividends for the bench  it would probably be me just because of  how bad my shoulder press was but  despite putting 90 pounds on it and  getting it up to a semi respectable  level it did absolutely nothing for my  bench press but I was never someone who  was limited by strength right off of my  chest  I could always you know get the bar  moving and then kind of missed through  that typical mid-range where most people  do so you know that's that's my own  experience with attempting to use the  overhead press to improve my bench  so I think kind of playing the long game  I think that if you have the type of  acromion process that that allows you to  tolerate overhead movements well that's  another video topic for another day but  essentially your chromium process of  your scapula kind of the roof of your  shoulder joint some people just have  more room in that joint than others and  so most people can overhead press fine  without issue other people that don't  have quite as much space in that joint  pretty much anything overhead is going  to be problematic or at least increase  their impingement risk but for most  people who can tolerate overhead  movements fine I do think that the  shoulder press is a good thing just to  have kind of in your base programming so  if you're a competitor and you're  offseason programming if you're you know  if you're just a bro just trying to live  to get jacked a good thing to keep in  your programming because it's a pressing  movement where your scapula aren't  pinned and in general that's probably  going to be better just for you know  shoulder health resilience in the long  run so similar movements would be like a  band resisted push-ups weighted dips etc  so I do think that it's a very good  movement in its own right  for that reason just helping you get  strong at pressing in general while also  you know maybe being a little bit better  for shoulder health for most people than  the bench press because your scapula  aren't pinned in that immobile position  but like I said for if you're if you're  looking for a movement specifically to  improve your bench press it's probably  not going to be the best accessory  movement for most people especially  because it it's also a pretty taxing  movement and will probably take away  from how hard you can train your bench  press however if you do miss just at the  very start of the press it's a great  movement and it's probably something  that if you add it to your training will  help your bench press so that was the  first addition I'll come up with a  snappy name for these videos at some  point not good at that but until next  time if you have a question just ask it  in the comments below or if you're  watching this on Facebook just that same  thing comment below and if I like your  question I will answer it in the next  video alright see you guys then
 

